{
  "name": "Is an Annual Roof Inspection Really Necessary for Texas Homeowners?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1: Inspect Roofing Materials",
      "text": "Your annual inspection should start with a close look at all roofing materials – shingles, tiles, or metal panels. The inspector will check for any signs of wear, such as cracking, curling, blistering, or missing pieces. For asphalt shingles, they’ll also look for excessive granule loss, which can indicate an aging roof losing its protective layer. This step aims to identify any surface damage that could compromise your roof’s integrity over time.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2: Examine Flashing and Penetrations",
      "text": "Next, the inspector will meticulously examine all flashing around chimneys, vents, skylights, and other roof penetrations. These are common weak points where water can get in if the seals are compromised or the flashing is damaged. They’ll look for corrosion, cracks, or gaps that could allow moisture to seep into your home, causing unseen damage.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3: Assess Gutters and Drainage System",
      "text": "Proper drainage is crucial for preventing water damage. The inspection will include a thorough check of your gutters and downspouts to ensure they are clear of debris, securely attached, and directing water away from your foundation. Blocked or damaged gutters can lead to water pooling on your roof or overflowing, causing fascia damage and other problems. This step helps ensure your entire water management system is functioning correctly.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4: Check Accessible Attic Areas",
      "text": "If accessible, the inspector will venture into your attic to look for any signs of leaks, water stains, mold, or poor ventilation. Attic checks can reveal issues that aren't visible from the exterior, such as condensation problems or subtle water ingress that hasn't yet made it to your living spaces. This internal examination offers valuable insights into your roof’s performance and overall structural health.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 5: Document Findings and Recommend Action",
      "text": "Finally, the professional will document all findings, providing you with a detailed report, often including photographs. They will clearly differentiate between normal wear, minor maintenance items, and more urgent repair needs. This report will help you understand the condition of your roof and what steps, if any, should be taken to maintain its health and extend its lifespan. They'll also explain the urgency of any findings. Ready to ensure your roof is ready for whatever Texas weather throws at it? Contact Discounted Roofing Co. for your annual roof inspection.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 5
    }
  ],
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "speakable": {
    "@type": "SpeakableSpecification",
    "cssSelector": [
      "h1",
      ".howto-step",
      "[itemprop=\"step\"]"
    ]
  },
